## Summary

### Root Cause
The `dequeue()` method in `src/queue.ts` (lines 35-38) called `this.queue.shift()` without checking if the queue was empty. On an empty queue, `shift()` returns `undefined`, which was then cast to `Job<T>` via `as Job<T>` — silently returning an invalid value instead of throwing an error.

### Selected Hypothesis
**`hyp-bdda42ee`**: `dequeue()` does not throw when queue is empty — it silently returns undefined cast to `Job<T>`. Fix: check queue length before shift and throw Error with message matching `/empty/i`.

### Files Changed
- **`src/queue.ts`** — Added an empty-queue guard at the start of `dequeue()`:
  ```typescript
  dequeue(): Job<T> {
      if (this.queue.length === 0) {
          throw new Error("Cannot dequeue from an empty queue");
      }
      const job = this.queue.shift();
      return job as Job<T>;
  }
  ```

### Verification
- **`npm test`** — All 9 tests pass (previously 1 failed: "dequeue throws on empty")
- **Git diff** — Clean, focused change: 1 file, +3 lines, -1 line

